Skill build_html_layout {
label: 'Build HTML Layout'

description: '''
Build, redesign, or fix the HTMLLayout presentation of a Holistics dashboard.

```
Use when `build_dashboard_v2` selects HTMLLayout, when the user explicitly requests HTMLLayout, or when an existing dashboard needs custom HTML/CSS presentation such as custom tabs/navigation, branded dashboard shells, narrative/asymmetric layouts, slides, paginated reports, or specialized embedded views.

This skill owns HTML structure, CSS composition, live-block placement, responsive/print behavior, and HTML-specific presentation.

It does not decide what analysis the dashboard should contain.

When no custom style is requested, use the Holistics default HTML style defined in this skill.

When a custom theme has been resolved by `build_dashboard_theme`, use that theme's design tokens as the visual direction for the HTML composition.

Do not use for ordinary native Canvas/Tab dashboards or theme-only changes.
```

'''

invocation: 'auto'
allow_switching_invocation: true

content: @md

# Building an HTML Layout

Build or edit the `HTMLLayout` presentation of an approved Holistics dashboard.

HTML/CSS owns presentation.

Holistics analytical blocks own live data and interactions.

Never hard-code dataset-derived values into HTML.

## What a good input looks like

Determine:

1. **Live blocks** — visualizations, KPIs, filters, and controls.
2. **Composition** — grouping, reading order, relative prominence.
3. **Format** — dashboard, custom tabs, slides, paginated report, embedded view, etc.
4. **Presentation behavior** — navigation, responsive behavior, print behavior, density.
5. **Visual direction** — Holistics default or an explicitly resolved custom theme.

When invoked from `build_dashboard_v2`, treat its approved composition contract as authoritative.

Do not restart analytical planning.

When editing an existing HTMLLayout, inspect it first and preserve unrelated behavior.

If the analytical structure is missing or materially wrong, use `build_dashboard_v2`.

## What a bare minimum output looks like

A valid HTMLLayout:

* preserves intended analytical blocks and interactions;
* places every intended live block exactly once;
* uses HTML/CSS only for presentation;
* contains no JavaScript;
* scopes CSS beneath one root class;
* gives visualizations explicit usable space;
* has an intentional visual system;
* works for its intended screen/navigation/print format;
* passes HTML preflight.

A technically valid but visually unfinished/basic HTMLLayout is not complete.

## Workflow

### 1. Read the approved composition

Establish:

* required live blocks;
* grouping;
* reading order;
* prominence;
* target format;
* navigation behavior;
* visual direction.

Do not add analytical blocks merely to fill space.

Do not remove or replace blocks because another composition is easier.

### 2. Resolve visual direction

Visual direction must be known before composing the HTML.

#### Holistics default

If no custom style was requested, use the Holistics default HTML visual system below.

Do not invoke `build_dashboard_theme`.

#### Custom theme

If `build_dashboard_theme` produced a custom theme, inspect its design tokens and use them as the visual direction.

Mirror relevant theme values into the HTML CSS token block where necessary.

Do not independently redesign the custom theme.

### 3. Choose or create a layout skeleton

If a suitable public HTMLLayout template is available, use it as a structural starting point.

Choose by:

* format;
* block count/types;
* grouping;
* navigation;
* responsive or paginated behavior.

Do not choose solely by aesthetic similarity.

Do not force the dashboard into an unsuitable template.

The approved composition contract always wins.

If no suitable template exists, build from scratch.

### 4. Establish the root and CSS tokens

Use one short, unique root class.

Scope all layout CSS beneath it.

Example:

```html
<div class="sales-dashboard">
  ...
</div>
```

Use CSS custom properties for reusable visual decisions.

## Holistics default HTML style

When no custom style is requested, use this visual direction.

```css
.dashboard-root {
  --t-bg: #FFFFFF;
  --t-surface: #F5F8FA;
  --t-border: #E1E7EE;

  --t-ink: #05264C;
  --t-ink-dim: #5C6E85;
  --t-ink-faint: #8FA0B4;

  --t-accent: #259B6C;
  --t-accent-lt: #3FBE88;
  --t-accent-dk: #1B7A54;

  --t-dark: #06213F;
  --t-dark-2: #0D3562;
  --t-dark-line: #1A4A80;

  --t-on-dark: #FFFFFF;
  --t-on-dark-dim: #D7E1EA;

  --t-radius: 10px;
  --t-radius-sm: 6px;
  --t-pad: 20px;

  --t-font-sans: Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
  --t-font-display: Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
  --t-font-mono: "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
}
```

Visual character:

* clean and minimal;
* light surfaces;
* dark navy text;
* restrained green accent;
* comfortable density;
* subtle borders rather than heavy shadows;
* clear typography hierarchy;
* accent used selectively, not as large page backgrounds.

These tokens style the HTML presentation.

Do **not** define a `ColorPalette` for the default Holistics style. Charts should continue to inherit Holistics' application-level default chart palette.

### 5. Apply an HTML-compatible PageTheme

HTMLLayout owns its outer surfaces, so an HTML-specific theme must not define page-level:

```aml
background { ... }
canvas { ... }
```

For the default Holistics HTML presentation, style Holistics-rendered blocks consistently while preserving the application's default chart palette.

Do not add `color { data: ... }` unless a custom chart palette was intentionally created.

At minimum, remove native block chrome:

```aml
block {
  background { bg_color: 'transparent' }

  border {
    border_width: 0
    border_style: 'none'
  }

  shadow: 'none'
  padding: 0
}
```

Also keep block typography, tables, KPIs, and rendered text aligned with the resolved visual direction.

When a custom theme exists, preserve its palette policy:

* custom palette defined → use it;
* no custom palette → inherit Holistics application palette.

### 6. Build the HTML structure

Render live blocks with:

```html
<h-block name="block_name" />
```

Wrap each block in an element you control.

```html
<section class="dashboard-root__hero">
  <div class="dashboard-root__chart">
    <h-block name="v_revenue_trend" />
  </div>
</section>
```

Never depend on styling `<h-block>` directly.

Each live block appears in exactly one `<h-block>`.

Use normal HTML for static presentation content such as:

* titles;
* labels;
* explanations;
* separators;
* repeated headers;
* repeated footers.

Dataset-derived content must remain in live Holistics blocks.

### 7. Build resilient CSS

Every visualization container needs explicit usable height.

For responsive layouts:

```css
.dashboard-root .hero-chart {
  min-height: 420px;
}
```

For fixed/paginated layouts, use a fixed height where needed.

Do not rely on `flex: 1` alone.

Use resilient grid tracks:

```css
grid-template-columns:
  minmax(0, 1fr)
  minmax(0, 1fr);
```

Use `min-width: 0` on constrained grid/flex children when needed.

Scope all selectors beneath the root class.

Do not use unscoped selectors such as:

```css
body
*
table
```

### 8. Build custom navigation without JavaScript

Never use JavaScript, `<script>`, or inline event handlers.

For custom tabs/slides/navigation, use CSS mechanisms such as `:target`, `:has()`, or static state classes.

When using `:target`, include a default state so content appears before the first interaction.

Do not duplicate live blocks across tabs, slides, or pages.

Repeated presentation content should be static HTML/CSS.

### 9. Handle paginated output when needed

For print/paginated formats:

* use explicit page dimensions;
* control page breaks;
* avoid splitting important figures;
* prevent an unnecessary trailing blank page.

If Holistics export behavior cannot be verified, state the limitation rather than assuming print behavior.

### 10. Verify and repair

Run the HTML preflight against the actual AML.

Fix failures before delivery.

## Schema

### HTMLLayout

```aml
view: HTMLLayout {
  content: @html
    <style>
      .dashboard-root {
        /* tokens */
      }

      .dashboard-root .section {
        /* scoped styles */
      }
    </style>

    <div class="dashboard-root">
      <div class="section">
        <h-block name="v_example" />
      </div>
    </div>
}
```

Check:

https://docs.holistics.io/reference/aml/html-layout

### Declaration order

AML is block-scoped.

Anything referenced by the Dashboard must be declared before use.

Do not invent syntax.

### HTML PageTheme

For HTMLLayout:

* omit page `background`;
* omit `canvas`;
* keep Holistics block chrome transparent;
* style labels/text;
* style tables when used;
* style KPIs;
* style rendered markdown/text;
* only override `color { data: ... }` when a custom chart palette is intentional.

## Conventions

### Do

* Treat the approved dashboard specification as authoritative.
* Use Holistics default HTML style when no custom style is requested.
* Use custom theme tokens when a custom theme was explicitly resolved.
* Keep live values in Holistics blocks.
* Use HTML for composition and static presentation.
* Scope CSS beneath one root.
* Style wrappers rather than `<h-block>`.
* Give visualizations explicit height.
* Use resilient grid/flex sizing.
* Preserve Holistics' default chart palette unless intentionally overridden.
* Check public docs rather than guessing AML syntax.

### Don't

* Don't invent data or metrics.
* Don't hard-code analytical values into HTML.
* Don't create a new arbitrary "basic theme."
* Don't create a ColorPalette just because HTMLLayout is being used.
* Don't use JavaScript.
* Don't style `<h-block>` directly.
* Don't duplicate live blocks.
* Don't use unscoped CSS.
* Don't force unsuitable templates.
* Don't fabricate asset URLs.
* Don't redefine the analytical dashboard.

## Preflight

Before delivery, verify:

* [ ] every intended live block appears exactly once
* [ ] every `<h-block>` references a declared block
* [ ] no dataset-derived values are hard-coded
* [ ] no JavaScript or inline event handlers
* [ ] all CSS is scoped
* [ ] `<h-block>` is not styled directly
* [ ] visualization containers have explicit usable height
* [ ] grid/flex contents cannot unexpectedly overflow
* [ ] visual tokens are used consistently
* [ ] Holistics default chart palette is preserved unless intentionally overridden
* [ ] referenced AML objects are declared before use
* [ ] HTML theme excludes `background` and `canvas`
* [ ] native block chrome is transparent
* [ ] custom navigation has a default state
* [ ] no live blocks are duplicated across pages/tabs/slides
* [ ] paginated layouts handle final-page breaks
* [ ] diagnostics contain no errors

;;
}
